        $add('[data-channel][data-property="value"]','channels.*.value','contact-channels','Destinations are the actual phone number, username, email or HTTPS URL opened by a channel.','Use country codes for phone-based services and usernames for Telegram or Messenger. Publish validates enabled methods.','WhatsApp: +12125550100; Telegram: yourusername; Custom URL: https://example.com/contact.','All channels off with empty destinations on a new installation.');
        $add('[data-path^="form_style."]','form_style.*','callback-form','Styles the readable callback and enquiry form separately from the floating buttons.','Keep text, fields and submit actions distinguishable against a solid form background. Reset this section restores form appearance only.','Use a white form background with dark text even when the expanded button stack is transparent.','White background, dark text, green submit button and rounded corners.');
        $add('[data-path^="launcher_style."]','launcher_style.*','floating-launcher','Styles the main control that opens your expandable contact menu.','Icon, colors and dimensions change the launcher only. Zero custom width or height keeps automatic sizing.','Use a phone handset icon and a descriptive Contact options label with visible keyboard focus.','Phone handset for fresh configurations while closed, X while open, dark background and soft shadow; saved custom closed icons remain selected.');
        $add('[data-path^="callback_style."]','callback_style.*','callback-form','Styles the visitor’s Request a Callback action separately from other contact buttons.','Placement and edge alignment affect where that action appears; form colors are controlled in their own section.','Place callback below the contact methods using a consistent green action.','Rounded green callback button below the contact methods.');
        $add('[data-field="consent"], [data-field="privacy_text"], [data-field="privacy_url"]','consent; privacy_text; privacy_url','privacy','Shows your disclosure and optional policy link with the enquiry form.','Requiring submission consent adds a checkbox before contact details can be sent. This does not grant analytics consent.','Explain that submitted details are used to respond to the enquiry and link your site’s policy.','Submission consent required; disclosure prepared; policy URL uses the website privacy policy if configured.');
        return $entries;
    }
    public static function topics() {
        return json_decode(<<<'JSON'
{
"overview":{"title":"Plugin overview and benefits","category":"Start here","keywords":["overview","benefits","free","local"],"what":"Floating Chat Buttons gives visitors direct routes to WhatsApp, Call, SMS, Telegram, Messenger, Email and a Custom URL, plus a callback or quick-enquiry form. Settings, a private enquiry inbox and optional aggregate Contact Analytics live in WordPress.","when":"Use it when visitors need a visible way to contact your business and your team needs a fallback when a direct conversation cannot begin immediately.","configure":"Open Contact Buttons → Easy Setup. New administrators begin with Simple mode: Phone number, WhatsApp number, Request a Callback, Design, Position and Publish. Choose Show Advanced Settings to reach every existing channel, appearance, availability, analytics and privacy control. The interface mode is a personal preference, not a change to your website configuration.","default":"The public widget starts disabled and all contact channels start unselected. Callback requests and private enquiry storage are prepared on. Analytics, GA4, sitewide recovery and weekly reports start off.","example":"A local service business offers WhatsApp, Call and Email, with a callback form for visitors who prefer a response later.","limits":"A contact-link click only opens the selected service. It cannot confirm that a call connected or that a message was sent. The plugin does not provide a live-chat agent service or install an external chat SDK.","troubleshoot":"If no widget appears, check that at least one channel has a valid destination, the widget is enabled and published, and the device, display rules and channel hours permit it."},
"getting-started":{"title":"Installation and getting started","category":"Start here","keywords":["install","activate","setup","zip","publish"],"what":"Installs the plugin and connects its administration pages to your WordPress website.","when":"Follow these steps on a new site or when installing the updated plugin ZIP.","configure":"1. In WordPress, open Plugins → Add New Plugin → Upload Plugin, choose the plugin ZIP, install and activate it.\n2. Open Contact Buttons → Easy Setup.\n3. In Simple mode, enable Call and/or WhatsApp and enter real numbers with country codes. Choose whether to offer Request a Callback.\n4. Choose Design and Position, inspect desktop and mobile previews and review the saved/published status.\n5. Choose Publish widget and check a public page. Advanced settings are optional for this basic setup.","default":"Activation prepares storage and scheduled maintenance. It does not publish a contact widget with invented business details.","example":"Enable Call and WhatsApp, enter +12125550100 for each applicable business destination, leave callback available, choose a design and publish without opening advanced settings.","limits":"The plugin requires WordPress 6.4 or later and PHP 7.4 or later. An administrator with manage_options permission manages settings. JavaScript is needed for the visual editor.","troubleshoot":"If the setup page does not respond, enable JavaScript and inspect browser errors. If an older cached widget remains visible after publishing, clear the website/page cache and check again."},
"simple-advanced-mode":{"title":"Simple versus Advanced settings","category":"Start here","keywords":["simple","advanced","administrator","preference","hidden settings"],"what":"Simple mode provides Call and WhatsApp numbers, independent labels, basic display styles and order; optional callback label/placement; basic appearance and position; and publishing controls. Basic Launcher/Close positioning appears only when the selected layout has additional expandable options. Advanced mode exposes every channel, full appearance, grouping, ordering, effects and Close positioning. Introductory bubble and basic mobile-bar palette controls remain available where relevant.","when":"Use Simple mode for a straightforward first setup. Use Advanced mode for additional contact methods, grouping, hours, page targeting, tracking, reports and detailed appearance.","configure":"Open Easy Setup and choose Show Advanced Settings to access the complete editor. Choose Use Simple Settings to return. The interface preference is stored for the current administrator; another administrator can choose a different mode without changing the site configuration.","default":"New administrators start in Simple mode. Changing mode saves only the interface preference and never modifies live/draft contact settings or deletes records.","example":"An administrator with Call, WhatsApp and Email enabled switches to Simple. Call and WhatsApp remain editable and Email appears in the additional-method summary. All three remain configured.","limits":"Simple mode does not override an existing advanced schedule, targeting rule, disabled device or tracking setting. It cannot serve as a reset of advanced configuration. Every existing setting remains accessible in Advanced mode.","troubleshoot":"If the preview seems affected by a hidden setting, choose Show Advanced Settings and inspect the relevant hours, targeting, device or channel controls. Check the mode indicator if advanced navigation is missing."},
"two-minute-setup":{"title":"Two-minute Call and WhatsApp setup","category":"Start here","keywords":["two minute","beginner","phone number","whatsapp number","quick setup"],"what":"The short Simple setup lets a beginner publish Call, WhatsApp and optional callback without configuring scheduling, analytics, recovery or targeting.","when":"Use this path for a new business contact widget or a quick update to the two main phone-based methods.","configure":"1. Open Contact Buttons → Easy Setup in Simple mode.\n2. Enable Call and/or WhatsApp, enter the numbers and optionally customize each label, basic display style and order.\n3. Set Request a Callback, its label and position if needed.\n4. Choose Floating buttons, Text buttons or Contact menu and your Position. Basic Launcher/Close controls appear only for an applicable expandable layout.\n5. Review the bubble for floating options or the four mobile-bar palette colors.\n6. Check the desktop/mobile preview, then Save draft or Publish widget. Advanced icons, effects, all-channel grouping and appearance remain optional.","default":"No real phone numbers are invented. The existing draft/publish system remains in use. Optional advanced collection, tracking and reports stay off unless already configured on the site.","example":"A shop enters its monitored Call and WhatsApp numbers, leaves callback available, chooses bottom right and publishes without opening Advanced mode.","limits":"The approximate two-minute goal assumes you already know your destinations and notification recipient. If the site already has advanced configuration, Simple preserves it rather than wiping it. Callback notifications use the existing site recipient and privacy configuration. The local Amit Jyoti Shaw preview is example website content, not your live site. No author branding, promotional links or credits are automatically added to the public widget.","troubleshoot":"If publish names an invalid method, correct its destination. If an additional enabled method needs attention, choose Show Advanced Settings. If visitors see an earlier configuration, confirm Publish widget succeeded and clear cached pages."},
"easy-setup":{"title":"Easy Setup in Simple and Advanced modes","category":"Start here","keywords":["wizard","steps","methods","details","preview"],"what":"Simple mode offers a short setup on the existing Easy Setup screen. Advanced mode retains the complete four-step wizard: Contact methods, Your details, Your design and Publish. Both edit the same draft.","when":"Use Easy Setup for a first configuration or to revisit your existing contact choices and presentation.","configure":"For a quick setup, fill Phone number and WhatsApp number, choose their enabled states, toggle Request a Callback, then choose Design and Position and Publish widget. Choose Show Advanced Settings for the complete wizard: select methods, enter details/reorder, choose presentation, then review and publish. Use Save draft to continue later without changing the public widget.","default":"Your website changes only when you publish. Preview links stay inside the editor and preview forms do not send requests.","example":"Configure Call and WhatsApp in Simple mode. Switch to Advanced to add Email, then return to Simple: Email is summarized and preserved even though its advanced controls are hidden.","limits":"Simple mode does not remove existing channels or turn off hours, targeting, analytics or reports. These settings can still affect the published widget; use Advanced mode when the existing configuration needs review. The original eight presets remain available, with a mobile contact-bar layout choice added.","troubleshoot":"If Next reports missing details, enter a valid destination for every selected channel. If a design uses one launcher, open its menu to inspect all selected contact methods."},
"contact-channels":{"title":"Adding and configuring contact channels","category":"Contact methods","keywords":["whatsapp","phone","email","telegram","messenger","sms","destination","order"],"what":"Each channel stores its enabled state, destination, label, colors, order, appearance and optional hours.","when":"Enable only the services your team monitors and keep destinations current.","configure":"In Contact Buttons or Easy Setup, select methods and fill in each destination. WhatsApp, Call and SMS accept a phone number with country code; Email needs a valid email address; Telegram needs a username without t.me/; Messenger needs a page username without m.me/; Custom URL requires HTTPS. Use the up/down reorder controls for button order. Save a draft or publish when ready. In Simple mode, Call and WhatsApp have direct controls and callback has its own toggle. Choose Show Advanced Settings to edit Email, Telegram, Messenger, SMS, Custom URL, labels, colors, order and schedules. Additional enabled channels remain visible in a summary when returning to Simple mode. Selected contact methods also have individual label, content style and ordering controls. Simple mode provides these basics for Call and WhatsApp; Advanced mode provides them for every supported channel.","default":"No channel is enabled on a new installation. Labels and service colors are prepared. Per-channel appearance inherits shared styling unless Use individual appearance is enabled.","example":"WhatsApp +12125550100 opens wa.me/12125550100; Call uses tel:; Email hello@example.com uses mailto:. A Telegram username can be entered as yourusername.","limits":"Phone destinations accept 7–15 digits after formatting is normalized. Telegram usernames must match the supported 5–32-character format. A Custom URL cannot contain embedded login credentials. A valid destination does not establish that the receiving account is active.","troubleshoot":"If publish rejects a method, check the destination format and remove a pasted service URL when a username is required. On a real device, verify that the intended phone or messaging app opens. Preview links intentionally do not navigate."},
"button-ordering":{"title":"Independent contact-button order","category":"Design","keywords":["order","move up","move down","selected methods","keyboard","locations"],"what":"The saved channel order controls the sequence of enabled contact methods. Moving a channel moves its whole record, preserving its destination, hours, icon and individual settings.","when":"Put the methods your team prefers first, and review where every selected method appears in direct, launcher and mobile-bar layouts.","configure":"In Simple Easy Setup, use the numbered Call/WhatsApp order indication and their Move Up / Move Down controls. In Advanced mode, Button order and locations lists every selected method with its current order and Desktop/Mobile location. Activate the same up/down controls by pointer or keyboard. Changes move whole records and update the preview immediately; Save draft or Publish widget when ready.","default":"The existing saved channel order is preserved on upgrade. Changing a design preset, grouping or position does not remove methods or replace their destinations.","example":"Move Email above WhatsApp in the selected-method list. If both belong inside More, Email appears earlier there; a direct Call action stays in its configured group.","limits":"Actual visible order includes only currently eligible actions: disabled, closed or device-hidden methods do not create blank slots. Group assignments decide whether a method is direct or additional. Callback placement and the floating Close control have separate controls. Reordering does not publish automatically.","troubleshoot":"If a method does not move where expected, inspect its direct/additional group and the relevant desktop/mobile layout. Check hours and device visibility before assuming it was removed. Use Advanced mode to inspect methods summarized but not fully editable in Simple mode."},
"individual-button-content":{"title":"Individual labels, icons and display styles","category":"Design","keywords":["text only","icon text","icon only","label","content mode","rtl","accessible name"],"what":"Each contact channel has its own label, icon and content display style. A local override affects that button rather than forcing every other channel to match.","when":"Use text-only for a clear written action, icon plus text for a recognisable service with a label, or icon-only when space is limited.","configure":"In Simple Easy Setup, edit Call button label or WhatsApp button label and its Call display style / WhatsApp display style. Choices are Use preset, Text only, Icon + Text and Icon only. In Advanced mode, a channel’s Button label, Display style, Icon and remaining appearance controls provide the full independent configuration. Check the preview before saving or publishing.","default":"The content override starts at inherit: the existing preset and saved legacy label preferences remain the starting point. New channel effects inherit the Easy Setup Water Ripple default; existing explicit choices are retained. Existing labels, selected icons, custom appearance and destinations are preserved on upgrade.","example":"Use Call Now as text-only for Call, Chat on WhatsApp as Icon + Text for WhatsApp, and an Email icon with the accessible label Send Email. Editing WhatsApp does not change Call or Email.","limits":"Icon-only buttons retain a meaningful accessible name from their channel label. Icon + Text places the icon before the text in the normal reading order with RTL handling. Selecting Text only does not delete the saved icon. The mobile-bar palette may still supply shared icon/text colors.","troubleshoot":"If a label appears hidden, check that channel’s Display style and legacy/preset inheritance. If its screen-reader name is unclear, use a descriptive label instead of leaving it blank. Inspect individual overrides before changing a shared preset."},
"per-button-animations":{"title":"Optional Water Ripple and Soft Pulse","category":"Design","keywords":["animation","water ripple","water drop","soft pulse","finite cycles","none","preview","motion"],"what":"Individual channel buttons can draw gentle attention with Water Ripple or Soft Pulse. The same effect implementation is used by the small settings preview and the published frontend buttons.","when":"Use a restrained effect for a priority contact route. Leave less important methods at None to avoid competing motion.","configure":"In Advanced Design Studio, open the channel’s individual appearance controls and choose Button animation: Use Easy Setup default, None, Water Ripple or Soft Pulse. Use Preview animation to inspect the small sample running the same effect implementation as the frontend. Review the actual public button as well, and save/publish explicitly.","default":"New configurations enable the Easy Setup Water Ripple default. Each channel can inherit it or explicitly choose None, Water Ripple or Soft Pulse. Existing saved preferences are unchanged. Published effects run at most two finite cycles per eligible button per page/effect choice instead of restarting on a perpetual timer.","example":"Choose Water Ripple for Call and None for Email, or use Soft Pulse on WhatsApp. Only the selected channel receives that effect; contact navigation remains immediate.","limits":"The two-cycle limit is fixed, not a repeat-interval control. Global Enable interaction animations, prefers-reduced-motion and hidden-page state stop unnecessary motion. Effects do not alter permanent button size, shift layout or intercept clicks. Existing launcher/form transitions are configured separately. No external animation library is required. An effect waits until its button is eligible and actually visible, including options revealed inside a launcher. Reopening the menu does not automatically restart a completed effect.","troubleshoot":"If the effect does not run, check the channel choice, global animation toggle, reduced-motion preference, page visibility and whether its finite cycles have already completed. Disabled or unavailable methods do not run an attention effect. Use the small preview to inspect a choice without assuming it will repeat continuously. Preview animation is an explicit replay for inspecting the choice; it does not enable a continuous frontend loop."},
"design-presets":{"title":"Design presets and appearance customization","category":"Design","keywords":["preset","appearance","colors","circle","pill","stack","minimal"],"what":"Presets set presentation: layout, shared appearance, spacing and launcher presentation. They preserve selected channels, destinations, channel order, hours, visibility, callback behavior and saved enquiries.","when":"Use a preset as a visual starting point, then adjust details to fit your website.","configure":"Open Design Studio or Easy Setup → Your design. Compare Call First + Callback, WhatsApp First + Call Fallback, WhatsApp + Call Separate Buttons, Expandable Multi-Contact, Compact Vertical Stack, Minimal Single Contact, Icon + Text Pill and Business Contact Panel. Adjust Appearance, Button layout, launcher label, size, spacing and advanced appearance. Check desktop and mobile previews before publishing. The Mobile Contact Bar preset selects the full-width mobile layout while preserving the desktop layout and all contact/business settings.","default":"Call First + Callback is the initial preset identifier. It does not select channels for a new installation. Shared circle styling begins at 56 px with a 12 px gap.","example":"A site using WhatsApp, Call and Email switches from separate buttons to a launcher design. The same three methods remain inside the expandable menu with their original destinations.","limits":"Preset names and thumbnail services illustrate a design. Optional methods are never silently enabled. Per-channel overrides can make a button differ from the shared preset. Designs do not delete customer records.","troubleshoot":"If a method seems absent, expand the launcher, check that method’s enabled flag, device visibility and hours, and review individual appearance. Use Restore this preset’s appearance to return its styling to the starting point. For grouped layouts, inspect both Always visible contact buttons and Additional contact options inside the launcher. The mobile-bar preset does not delete methods that belong inside More."},
"floating-launcher":{"title":"Floating launcher","category":"Design","keywords":["launcher","expandable","collapse","label","icon"],"what":"An expandable launcher opens additional contact methods; it never starts a telephone call. Its closed icon identifies the control, and opening the menu changes that icon to a clear X Close. Direct Call remains a separate tel: action.","when":"Use a launcher to keep several contact routes compact, especially on small screens.","configure":"Choose Expandable contact panel or configure per-device contact grouping. In Design Studio, expand Expandable launcher to set icon, label, accessibility text, colors, dimensions, radius, shadow and focus color. Configure Start expanded separately for desktop and mobile in Display & Visibility. The mobile bar labels its additional-options control More. In supported floating layouts, adjust the Close control’s position within the additional stack. Top or intermediate placement reserves space from the first display to keep its anchor stable. The mobile-bar More control stays in the bar.","default":"New configurations use the existing clean phone handset asset while closed and X while expanded, with accessible state labels Open contact options and Close contact options. The short icon transition respects reduced motion. Saved custom closed icons remain selected; the legacy contact icon alias renders the clean handset.","example":"Select Open contact options to reveal Email and Callback, then use the X to close the menu. A separate Call action dials immediately. The introductory bubble hides while the launcher is open by default and returns when it closes.","limits":"Launcher styling is separate from the expanded container and the callback form. Zero custom width or height means automatic sizing. Other floating website elements can overlap it unless you leave enough edge spacing.","troubleshoot":"If the launcher opens but few methods appear, check enabled channels, hours and device visibility. If it overlaps a cookie banner, adjust the desktop/mobile spacing in Display & Visibility."},
"launcher-close-position":{"title":"Floating Launcher/Close position","category":"Design","keywords":["close","launcher index","top","bottom","stack","anchor","more","placement"],"what":"The launcher becomes X Close when the additional floating options are open. Its position within that additional stack can be adjusted independently for desktop and mobile floating layouts.","when":"Use top, bottom or an intermediate stack position when it makes the Close control easier to reach without confusing it with a direct contact action.","configure":"In Simple mode, Launcher / Close position offers Top of additional buttons or Bottom of additional buttons for the device currently shown in preview; unsupported layouts hide these controls. In Advanced mode, each device has Launcher / Close position with Top, intermediate After positions and Bottom, plus Move Close control up/down controls. The numeric position counts eligible additional actions before Close, in top-to-bottom order. Save draft or publish after reviewing both devices.","default":"The stored launcher_index is -1 for bottom, 0 for top, or a positive position within the additional stack. Existing layouts retain bottom placement. Runtime placement is clamped to the available actions.","example":"With Email and Callback inside Contact options, put Close at the top or between those additional actions. The always-visible Call group remains direct and the mobile bar keeps More in its fixed row.","limits":"Close placement applies to supported floating stacks; mobile-bar More remains in the bar. Top/intermediate placement reserves potential menu space below the Close anchor from the first display, so a closed launcher can start higher. This prevents position changes during opening/closing. Always visible bubbles reserve their own predictable space above the potential stack. A launcher with no additional actions stays hidden. In a horizontal direction, channel rows remain horizontal/wrapped inside the sections before and after Close; Close separates those sections rather than becoming an inline contact action.","troubleshoot":"If Close seems higher while the menu is closed, that is the reserved anchor space; choose bottom placement for a compact closed layout. If a saved intermediate index is beyond the eligible actions, runtime clamps it. Check the per-device group, callback placement and current hours before changing the index."},
"introductory-bubble":{"title":"Introductory bubble text and visibility","category":"Design","keywords":["bubble","intro","how can we help","hide on open","welcome"],"what":"The introductory bubble is anchored independently of the expanding menu. Opening or closing the launcher does not push it through the contact-button layout.","when":"Use a brief helpful invitation when the floating launcher would otherwise be easy to miss. Turn it off when it would distract from your page.","configure":"In Easy Setup, set Show introductory bubble, Bubble text and Bubble behavior: Always visible, Hide when launcher opens or Never show. Hide when launcher opens completes the bubble exit before revealing menu actions. Closing completes the menu collapse before the bubble reappears. Rapid Open/Close changes cancel the outdated transition and settle to the latest state. Save draft or publish after reviewing the preview.","default":"The bubble is enabled with How can we help? and Hide when launcher opens. Its position stays anchored during both transitions. Mobile bars omit the floating bubble.","example":"Open a bottom-right Contact options launcher: the invitation leaves first, then the additional buttons appear. Close it: the buttons finish collapsing before the invitation returns at the same anchored position.","limits":"Always visible reserves space above the potential expanded stack from the first display, so it can appear above the closed launcher without jumping or overlapping menu controls. A non-bottom Close position also reserves room for actions below that control. Choose Hide when launcher opens and bottom Close placement for a more compact closed appearance. Off, Never show or empty text hides the bubble. Hidden content does not intercept clicks or keyboard focus. Reduced motion skips unnecessary movement; mobile bars stay uncluttered.","troubleshoot":"If the closed invitation appears higher than expected, inspect Always visible and the Close position: reserved space keeps the anchor stable. Switch to Hide when launcher opens or bottom Close placement for compactness. If absent, check its checkbox, text, behavior and active mobile-bar layout. Test rapid opening/closing as well as resting states before publishing."},
"launcher-meaning":{"title":"Direct Call versus Open/Close contact options","category":"Design","keywords":["launcher","handset","close icon","aria label","open contact options","more","call"],"what":"Call starts a telephone action immediately. The launcher only opens or closes contact options: a clean handset appears while closed and a clear X appears while expanded. Its Open contact options / Close contact options accessibility labels explain that distinction.","when":"Use a descriptive launcher label and accessibility text whenever the control opens multiple contact routes.","configure":"In Advanced Design Studio, set Launcher label. In Expandable launcher, choose Icon and Launcher accessibility text. The new handset uses the existing Call icon asset. Keep the visible launcher label about options rather than an instruction to dial; mobile-bar menus retain More.","default":"New configurations use a phone handset while closed and X while open. Existing saved custom icons are preserved for the closed state; the old contact icon selection now renders a clean handset without rewriting its saved value. Opening always shows X.","example":"A Call channel opens tel: immediately. The separate Contact options launcher expands the menu and changes to X. Screen readers hear Open contact options while closed and Close contact options while expanded.","limits":"A handset selected for the launcher does not make it a call link. Keep a descriptive options label and a separately labelled direct Call action. The expanded launcher shows X and the visible label Close. Meaningful saved Launcher accessibility text is appended after the state phrase, for example Open contact options: Sales desk and Close contact options: Sales desk. Default names are exactly Open contact options / Close contact options. Reduced-motion preferences remove unnecessary icon transitions.","troubleshoot":"If a saved custom icon remains while closed, that is intentional. Choose Call in the launcher Icon setting to use the new handset explicitly. The old contact icon alias also renders the handset. Check the control’s role and open/close state rather than assuming every phone graphic dials."},
"button-positioning":{"title":"Button positioning and responsive layouts","category":"Design","keywords":["position","left","right","mobile","desktop","gap","safe area"],"what":"Desktop and mobile each have their own placement, layout, button direction, size, icon size, gap and initial expanded state.","when":"Use independent settings when your mobile navigation or consent banner needs different spacing from desktop.","configure":"Open Display & Visibility → Independent desktop and mobile layouts. Set Show on desktop and Show on mobile. Within each device group, choose Placement, Layout and Button direction, then adjust Horizontal edge spacing, Bottom spacing, Button size, Icon size and Gap between buttons. Use the two preview viewport controls to compare. For a full-width sticky mobile layout, choose the mobile contact bar and configure its top/bottom edge, breakpoint and optional content-safe spacing. The desktop layout remains independent.","default":"Both devices are enabled. Desktop uses bottom right with 24 px edge spacing and 56 px buttons. Mobile uses bottom right with 16 px spacing and 50 px buttons. Both use vertical direction and 12 px gaps.","example":"Place the desktop widget bottom right, but put mobile bottom left with extra bottom spacing above a sticky booking bar.","limits":"Safe-area spacing is included. Actual layout also depends on your website font, content, individual button dimensions and available viewport size. Long labels and large widths can consume mobile space.","troubleshoot":"Reduce oversized individual widths or labels if content feels crowded. Check both published device settings, avoid excessive padding, and inspect the actual public page for conflicts with other sticky widgets."},
"mobile-contact-bar":{"title":"Full-width mobile contact bar","category":"Design","keywords":["mobile bar","sticky","call","whatsapp","more","callback","equal width"],"what":"The mobile contact bar is a full-width sticky row of equal-width contact actions, rather than a full-screen overlay. Its prepared direct actions are Call and WhatsApp when available; explicit grouping can choose different direct methods. Direct actions open their configured destinations; More opens the additional selected methods.","when":"Use it when mobile visitors benefit from prominent icon-and-short-label actions along one viewport edge.","configure":"Choose Position → Mobile contact bar in Simple mode or the Mobile Contact Bar preset. In Advanced Design Studio, Mobile layout offers Full-width mobile contact bar. Under Mobile contact bar → Bar placement and behavior, set Mobile bar position, Mobile breakpoint (px), Additional action and Reserve space for the mobile bar. Simple mode exposes four mobile-bar colors and Reset to preset colors. Advanced Mobile bar appearance retains bar/button backgrounds, separate icon/text colors, Border color, Border width (px), Padding (px) and Minimum action height (px). Review the mobile preview and a real mobile page, then publish.","default":"The existing floating mobile layout remains the default. When the bar is selected, its prepared edge is bottom, breakpoint is 767 px, minimum action height is 60 px, padding is 8 px and Additional action is More menu. Reserve space for the mobile bar is off.","example":"Call | WhatsApp | More dials directly, opens WhatsApp directly, and expands Email, Telegram, SMS, Messenger and Callback when those options are enabled and available. With only two direct methods there is no empty More slot.","limits":"Disabled or unavailable methods do not create empty slots. The same method appears once in the active layout. Direct Callback may replace More when it is the only remaining option; other additional methods must remain reachable. The bar follows the existing visibility and business-hours rules.","troubleshoot":"If an expected action is missing, inspect enabled methods, destinations, schedules and device visibility. If More is absent, check whether there are any additional available options. If it covers page controls, enable content-safe spacing or change its edge and verify your theme."},
"mobile-bar-colors":{"title":"Mobile contact-bar colors and palette reset","category":"Design","keywords":["bar background","button background","icon color","text color","reset preset colors"],"what":"Four Simple mode colors separately style the bar surface, action surfaces, icons and visible button labels. Individual advanced channel styling remains available separately.","when":"Use these controls to match the mobile bar to your website while keeping icons, text and focus states readable.","configure":"In Simple Easy Setup, choose Mobile contact bar and edit Contact bar background color, Button background color, Button icon color and Button text color. Changes update the current preview immediately. Reset to preset colors restores only those four palette values. Save draft for later or Publish widget to change the website.","default":"Fresh bar and button backgrounds are #172e2a; fresh icon and text colors are #ffffff. When upgrading a saved bar without separate button/icon colors, button background inherits its saved bar background and icon color inherits its saved text color.","example":"Use a dark bar background, a distinct button background and light icon/text colors. Reset to preset colors returns the four palette values to the preset baseline while keeping your numbers, grouping and bar position.","limits":"Palette edits and reset remain unpublished draft changes. Reset does not alter layout, destinations, button grouping, schedules, bar border, padding, minimum height or enquiries. Button text color affects labels when they are shown; advanced per-channel styling may need separate review.","troubleshoot":"If the public page has old colors, publish the reviewed draft and clear page cache. If an upgraded design appears unchanged, inheritance intentionally preserved its existing appearance. Select the mobile preview to see bar colors and check contrast after any palette change."},
"mobile-bar-position":{"title":"Mobile bar top/bottom edge and content spacing","category":"Design","keywords":["top","bottom","safe area","content spacing","toolbar","sticky bar"],"what":"The mobile bar can attach to the top or bottom of the mobile viewport. Safe-area handling keeps actions clear of device cutouts; optional content-safe spacing helps avoid covering important page controls.","when":"Choose the edge that works with your website header, sticky navigation, cookie banner and bottom browser controls.","configure":"In Advanced Design Studio → Mobile contact bar → Bar placement and behavior, choose Mobile bar position: Bottom of mobile screen or Top of mobile screen. Enable Reserve space for the mobile bar when needed. Under Mobile bar appearance, adjust Minimum action height (px) and Padding (px). Test scrolling and mobile browser toolbar changes on a real device, then publish.","default":"Bottom edge, 60 px minimum action height and 8 px padding are prepared. Reserve space for the mobile bar starts off. Theme templates are not rewritten.","example":"A site with a sticky bottom booking action puts the contact bar at the top; another site uses bottom with content-safe spacing so the final form submit button stays reachable.","limits":"Existing theme sticky controls can still conflict. Content spacing is an optional runtime accommodation, not an unnecessary permanent theme-template spacer. Safe-area size depends on the device and browser.","troubleshoot":"If a page control is obscured, compare top and bottom, enable spacing and review theme stacking/positioning. Check the published page instead of assuming editor preview reproduces every theme element."},
"mobile-breakpoint":{"title":"Mobile bar breakpoint and desktop independence","category":"Design","keywords":["breakpoint","responsive","width","desktop","mobile preview"],"what":"The mobile breakpoint decides the viewport width at which the mobile layout is used. Desktop retains its separate floating layout and grouping configuration.","when":"Adjust it when your site transitions between tablet/mobile and desktop navigation at a different width.","configure":"In Advanced Design Studio → Mobile contact bar → Bar placement and behavior, set Mobile breakpoint (px). Configure Desktop contact grouping and Mobile contact grouping separately. Compare widths on both sides of the breakpoint and use the preview viewport switcher to inspect the actual responsive widget.","default":"The prepared mobile breakpoint is 767 px. Choosing the mobile-bar preset changes the mobile layout and preserves the desktop layout.","example":"A website with mobile navigation below 768 px uses the prepared 767 px breakpoint; Call and WhatsApp form a mobile row while desktop keeps its compact floating stack.","limits":"Viewport width, rather than a device brand or user-agent name, determines the responsive layout. A tablet or narrow desktop browser can therefore use the mobile layout. Page-level theme behavior remains independent.","troubleshoot":"If the bar appears at an unexpected size, compare the browser viewport width with the published breakpoint and check Show on mobile/desktop. Verify that the mobile layout is actually selected and clear stale cached pages."},
"channel-grouping":{"title":"Always-visible and additional contact options","category":"Design","keywords":["grouping","always visible","additional","launcher","more","desktop","mobile"],"what":"Per-device grouping separates Always visible contact buttons from Additional contact options inside the launcher. Assignment changes presentation without deleting the selected methods, destinations or order.","when":"Use grouping when the most important methods should stay visible while secondary methods remain available through Contact options or More.","configure":"In Advanced Design Studio → Always visible buttons and additional options, open Desktop contact grouping or Mobile contact grouping. Enable Choose always-visible buttons for Desktop/Mobile, select the corresponding Desktop/Mobile layout and choose Always visible contact buttons. Remaining enabled methods are additional options inside Contact options / More. Review the preview; if no options remain inside the launcher, it hides automatically. Review the selected-method order/location list to see which enabled methods are direct and which appear inside the launcher. Use Move Up and Move Down to reorder whole channel records without changing their destinations.","default":"Grouping is off for existing floating layouts, preserving accepted earlier behavior. The mobile bar adapts intelligently with Call and WhatsApp direct when available and the other selected options inside More unless explicit grouping assigns them differently.","example":"Desktop keeps Call always visible and Email/Callback in Contact options. Mobile keeps Call and WhatsApp visible with Email/Callback under More.","limits":"An active method must not be duplicated between the direct group and menu. Disabled, closed or device-hidden methods do not create blank slots. Grouping preserves channel order and does not bypass business-hours or display rules. Close placement applies within the additional floating stack; it does not move the mobile-bar More slot.","troubleshoot":"If the launcher disappears, confirm whether any additional available options remain. If a method seems missing, check its enabled state, hours and the correct device group before changing its destination."},
"expanded-panel":{"title":"Optional expanded contact panel","category":"Design","keywords":["transparent","panel","background","padding","shadow","compact"],"what":"The expanded contact container groups the buttons. An optional styled panel can add a visible background around this group.","when":"Keep the container transparent for a clean floating stack. Enable a styled panel only when a grouped surface improves contrast against your page.","configure":"In Design Studio, find the expanded contact panel controls. Choose Enable panel background and adjust Background, Border Color, Radius, Padding and Shadow. Keep the dimensions compact and review the left/right edge alignment on both previews.","default":"The optional styled panel is disabled. The expanded contact buttons use a compact transparent container. The callback/enquiry form retains its own readable white background.","example":"Leave individually colored WhatsApp, Call and Email buttons floating without a large white rectangle behind them. For a busy photo page, enable a modest contrasting panel.","limits":"Panel styling affects the expanded contact group only. It does not change the form surface or replace per-button background controls. Transparent empty areas should allow interaction with the page beneath.","troubleshoot":"If you see an unwanted background, check the optional panel setting and individual button styles. Reset the panel section and clear page caches after publishing if an older design persists."},
"animations":{"title":"Interaction animations and reduced motion","category":"Design","keywords":["animation","motion","duration","stagger","escape","keyboard"],"what":"Subtle opacity and transform transitions soften opening and closing the launcher and callback form, with optional staggered contact-button reveals. Independent channel attention effects are optional and separate from menu/form interaction transitions.","when":"Keep short transitions for comfortable visual feedback; disable animations when you prefer immediate state changes.","configure":"In Design Studio, open Interaction animations. Use Enable interaction animations, Duration (ms) and Stagger contact button entrances. Review launcher open/close, callback open/close and alternative contact actions with a keyboard and touch or pointer.","default":"Animations are enabled with short transitions around 200 ms. The visitor’s prefers-reduced-motion preference takes priority and suppresses unnecessary motion.","example":"Open the launcher with a short fade/slide and a slight button stagger, then close it without a bounce or a long wait.","limits":"Animations never establish whether a contact action succeeded. Telephone and messaging navigation must remain immediate, and validation still runs normally. A reduced-motion preference may make a configured animation appear absent. Water Ripple and Soft Pulse follow this global animation setting as well as reduced-motion and visible-page checks, and each eligible button is limited to two cycles per page/effect choice.","troubleshoot":"If movement appears missing, inspect the operating-system reduced-motion preference and Enable interaction animations. If transitions feel too slow, reduce duration or stagger and review the actual published widget."},
"reset-controls":{"title":"Reset controls and safe draft changes","category":"Design","keywords":["reset","defaults","restore","unsaved","draft"],"what":"Reset this section restores a design group’s defaults. Reset design restores appearance across design groups. Restore this preset’s appearance reapplies the selected preset’s visual starting point. Reset draft restores the published configuration. Reset to preset colors restores only the four mobile-bar palette values.","when":"Use a scoped reset to undo a design experiment without rebuilding contact details or customer follow-up records.","configure":"Choose the reset beside the section you want to change, inspect the updated preview and unsaved-change status, then Save draft or Publish widget. Read the confirmation for Reset draft because it discards the draft’s differences from the published configuration.","default":"No reset runs automatically. Section and design resets change the editor draft and preview; they do not silently publish. Saved enquiries are not deleted by design resets.","example":"Reset Callback and enquiry form to regain a white form surface while retaining your enabled Call and Email destinations.","limits":"A section reset restores that section’s defaults rather than the last saved custom design. Reset draft can replace unpublished contact edits with their published values. Permanent inbox deletion is a separate explicit action. Reset to preset colors leaves bar placement, layout, grouping, border, height, padding, destinations and customer records unchanged. Individual appearance resets can restore that channel’s default content/effect presentation without deleting its destination, schedule or follow-up records. Order and layout changes remain draft edits until explicitly published.","troubleshoot":"If visitors still see the old appearance after a reset, publish the reviewed draft. If a draft change disappears, distinguish Reset draft from a section reset and inspect the saved/published state."},
"draft-published":{"title":"Draft versus Published","category":"Start here","keywords":["save","publish","live","draft","unsaved"],"what":"The plugin keeps a saved draft separately from the published configuration used by the public website.","when":"Use a draft to review design, targeting, privacy and integrations before changing the live experience.","configure":"Save draft stores current editor settings for later. Publish widget validates and stores the current configuration as both draft and published settings. Save & exit saves before leaving. Check the save-status message before closing the page.","default":"Edits, imported settings and section resets remain unpublished. Public report schedules and frontend behavior use published settings.","example":"Save an experimental mobile layout today while your current published layout stays live; publish it after reviewing tomorrow.","limits":"Preview interactions send no enquiry or analytics events. A toggle on the dashboard or WordPress toolbar can change widget enabled state directly. Reset draft restores the published configuration. Switching Simple/Advanced changes only your administrator interface preference; it never publishes or rewrites a site setting.","troubleshoot":"If a change is visible only in preview, publish it. If saving fails, inspect the field named in the error and retry after correcting it. Clear page caches when a successfully published change is not yet visible."},
"callback-form":{"title":"Callback request form and quick enquiries","category":"Contact methods","keywords":["callback","form","enquiry","name","message","consent"],"what":"The form collects a phone number or email so your team can respond later. Form type distinguishes a Callback request from a Quick enquiry in retained records and reports.","when":"Offer a form when visitors cannot use a direct channel immediately or need to explain their request.","configure":"Open Privacy & Mail. Enable Offer callback requests, choose Form type, and adjust Form heading, Form introduction and Submit button label. Optionally enable Ask for name and Ask for a message. Set the privacy disclosure, policy URL, submission consent and notification recipient. In Design Studio, Callback button and Callback and enquiry form control appearance. In Business Hours, Callback form hours sets independent availability. Simple mode exposes Callback button label and Callback button position: Above contact buttons or Below contact buttons. The label uses the existing Submit button label value, so the callback action and form submit button share it; no duplicate form or customer storage is created.","default":"Callback requests are enabled with one required phone-or-email field. Name and message are optional extras and start off. Submission consent and private storage start on. Callback hours are unrestricted.","example":"A visitor selects Request a Callback, leaves +12125550100 and optionally a short description, then the team follows up from the private inbox.","limits":"There is no automatic outbound call or guaranteed response time. If local storage is off and mail transport fails, the visitor receives an error instead of a false success. The server enforces callback hours and input validation.","troubleshoot":"If submission is rejected, enter a valid contact, grant required submission consent and check that callback hours are open. Check the private inbox and notification transport separately; see Callback notifications for email checks."},
"callback-notifications":{"title":"Callback notifications and email delivery","category":"Contact methods","keywords":["notification","email","mail","smtp","test","delivery"],"what":"Accepted form submissions are sent to the configured notification email using WordPress mail. The message contains the submitted customer information.","when":"Configure a monitored recipient whenever you want alerts for new callback requests or enquiries.","configure":"In Privacy & Mail, set Send notifications to. Choose Send test email, then check that recipient’s inbox and spam folder. Publish changes to the recipient. Configure your WordPress mail transport separately if your host cannot reliably send mail.","default":"The WordPress administrator email is prepared as the recipient. Private enquiry storage is on for new installations, so retained requests remain available even when notifications fail.","example":"Send alerts to a shared service inbox, with private records available in Customer Enquiries for follow-up.","limits":"A transport-accepted status means WordPress accepted the message for sending; it never confirms inbox delivery. The test action is limited to one attempt per five minutes per administrator. Email copies have their own retention.","troubleshoot":"Check the recipient address, WordPress mail transport, spam folder and private inbox notification status. If storage is off, enable it if you need independent local records. Never assume a successful test message guarantees every future delivery."},
"enquiry-inbox":{"title":"Private enquiry inbox and follow-up statuses","category":"Follow-up","keywords":["inbox","requests","status","new","contacted","resolved","csv"],"what":"Customer Enquiries is a private administrative inbox for saved customer contact details, source information, follow-up status and notification transport state.","when":"Use it to track responses independently of optional analytics and email delivery.","configure":"Enable Store enquiries in the private inbox under Privacy & Mail. Open Customer Enquiries or the Customer Enquiries navigation tab. Search or filter retained requests, update follow-up status, export filtered records to CSV or delete records using the provided actions.","default":"Storage is enabled on new installations. Previously disabled installations keep that choice. New requests begin with New status; Contacted and Resolved support follow-up.","example":"After calling a customer, change New to Contacted. Once their request is handled, mark it Resolved so unresolved counts reflect the remaining work.","limits":"The inbox contains only records successfully stored and not deleted by retention or manual actions. CSV files and email copies are outside the plugin’s later deletion controls. Changing status does not contact the customer automatically.","troubleshoot":"If a request is missing, confirm storage was enabled at submission time and review search, status filters and retention. Analytics totals are not an exact inventory of inbox records because consent and retention are independent."},
"display-rules":{"title":"Display rules and URL include/exclude patterns","category":"Availability","keywords":["visibility","targeting","url","include","exclude","homepage","wildcard"],"what":"Display & Visibility decides which public website views may show the widget. It supports content IDs, post types, templates, taxonomy terms, archives and URL patterns.","when":"Use targeting to keep the widget relevant or avoid sensitive and busy pages.","configure":"In Display & Visibility, choose Across the public website, Homepage only or Selected pages and views. Add inclusions and exclusions using the named search controls. Enter URL patterns one per line. Use Explain visibility for a website URL → Explain current draft to inspect unpublished rules before publishing.","default":"The widget may appear across the public website, subject to enabled devices, published status and contact availability. No inclusions or exclusions are prepared. Administration and login screens stay hidden.","example":"Select /services/* as an included path and /services/private/* as an excluded path. The excluded view stays hidden even when an inclusion matches.","limits":"Exclusions always take priority. Selected mode needs at least one matching inclusion. An asterisk matches any characters; use precise paths or full same-website URLs and account for URL queries. This is visibility control, not access control for page content.","troubleshoot":"Use Explain current draft with the exact public URL. Check mode, exclusions and device settings. If the explanation differs from the public site, publish the draft and clear cached page output."},
"page-templates":{"title":"Page, post and custom-template targeting","category":"Availability","keywords":["template","post type","taxonomy","archive","theme","page"],"what":"Template targeting matches the WordPress template assigned to content. Other selectors match actual pages/posts, public post types, taxonomy terms and archive views.","when":"Use templates to target a reusable page layout rather than maintaining a long list of individual page titles.","configure":"In Display & Visibility, choose Selected pages and views if inclusions should control visibility. Search Include Templates or Exclude Templates and select a result. Use the relevant Pages, posts and public content, Post types, Categories, tags and taxonomy terms, or Archive views selector for other targeting needs.","default":"No template targeting. Available template choices are derived from the active theme; the default template can also be targeted when available in the list.","example":"Include a landing-page template used by several campaign pages and exclude your checkout page by its content entry.","limits":"Stored template identifiers depend on the theme. Changing themes can make old selections unavailable. Template rules apply to assigned content templates, not every visual component used to build a page.","troubleshoot":"Confirm the page’s assigned template in WordPress. Revisit selections after a theme change, and use Explain current draft on a real URL. Use content or URL rules when the page builder does not expose a distinct WordPress template."},
"business-hours":{"title":"Business hours and channel availability","category":"Availability","keywords":["hours","schedule","interval","availability","weekday","closed"],"what":"Each contact method and the callback form can have independent opening intervals and a closed-hours action. Schedules do not remove saved channels or destinations.","when":"Restrict channels that only receive responses during staffed hours, while leaving asynchronous routes available longer.","configure":"Open Business Hours and expand the relevant method. Enable Use contact hours, choose Timezone, then Add interval. Select starting weekdays and 24-hour Opens and Closes times. Add multiple intervals for split shifts. Choose When this channel is closed. Configure Callback form hours separately if needed, then publish.","default":"Restrictions are off for every method and callback. Prepared intervals are Monday–Saturday, 09:00–18:00 in the website timezone; the closed action is Hide the button or Hide the callback form.","example":"Call is open weekdays 09:00–17:00, WhatsApp stays unrestricted, and callback requests remain available at all times.","limits":"Enabled restrictions with no intervals mean always closed. Opening and closing times cannot be identical; disable restrictions for 24-hour availability. Up to 100 intervals are supported per method. Callback cannot replace itself as a closed-hours alternative.","troubleshoot":"Check the chosen timezone, starting weekdays, intervals and closed action. Check whether an alternative method is both enabled and open. Cached frontend markup may need refreshing after published schedule changes."},
"business-hours-timezone":{"title":"Business-hours timezone and website timezone","category":"Availability","keywords":["timezone","site","wordpress","utc","iana","daylight saving","dst"],"what":"Use website timezone uses the timezone configured in WordPress → Settings → General. Your contact buttons follow their hours in that timezone. A separate IANA city timezone can be selected for a method instead.","when":"Use the website timezone for one location. Choose a separate city when a contact team works in another region.","configure":"In Business Hours, expand a method and choose its Timezone. The website option shows the actual configured timezone and current UTC offset. Select an IANA city such as Europe/London for independent hours. To change the website-wide timezone, open WordPress Settings → General.","default":"Every schedule uses the website timezone. Contact Analytics daily dates and weekly email schedules also use the website timezone, independently of a channel’s separate timezone.","example":"A website configured as Asia/Kolkata shows UTC+05:30. A support channel using Europe/London follows London wall time instead, including its daylight-saving changes.","limits":"A city timezone follows daylight-saving transitions; a fixed WordPress UTC offset does not. The displayed UTC offset is current and may change seasonally for a city. Channel timezone changes do not change report or analytics date boundaries.","troubleshoot":"Compare WordPress Settings → General with the schedule’s Timezone. Prefer the appropriate city instead of a fixed offset where daylight saving applies. Review hours after changing the website timezone."},
"overnight-schedules":{"title":"Overnight and split schedules","category":"Availability","keywords":["overnight","midnight","split shift","opens","closes","interval"],"what":"An interval whose closing time is earlier than its opening time continues into the following calendar day. Selected weekdays are the days the interval starts.","when":"Use this for late opening, overnight support or shifts that span midnight. Use multiple intervals for lunch breaks.","configure":"In Business Hours, select the starting weekday and enter the opening and closing times. Add another interval for a second shift or a different weekday range. A method is open when any interval matches.","default":"The prepared schedule is a daytime interval. No overnight interval is added automatically; restrictions remain off until enabled.","example":"Select Friday, Opens 22:00 and Closes 02:00. The method is open Friday 22:00–midnight and Saturday midnight–02:00. For a lunch break, add 09:00–12:00 and 13:00–17:00.","limits":"The opening instant is included and the closing instant is excluded. Equal opening and closing times are rejected. Overlapping intervals form a combined open period rather than taking precedence over one another.","troubleshoot":"If Saturday early morning seems missing, check Friday as the starting day. If a lunch break still appears open, check for another overlapping interval. Review timezone separately."},
"channel-closed-behavior":{"title":"What happens when a channel is closed","category":"Availability","keywords":["closed","hide","unavailable","alternative","callback"],"what":"Closed-hours behavior controls the visitor-facing action when a restricted channel is outside its intervals.","when":"Use a helpful fallback when hiding the channel would leave visitors without a suitable contact route.","configure":"In the method’s Business Hours group, choose Hide the button, Show unavailable message, Offer callback request or Offer another channel. Set Unavailable message and Alternative channel as needed. Callback-form closed behavior supports hiding, a message or another channel.","default":"Closed methods are hidden. If a requested alternative is disabled, closed or invalid, an unavailable message is used rather than a broken action.","example":"Outside Call hours, offer callback while its independent form hours remain open; otherwise provide the configured unavailable message.","limits":"Callback must be enabled before a channel uses Offer callback request. An alternative channel must be different, enabled and open. Callback cannot be its own alternative. This changes availability presentation without rewriting the channel’s saved destination.","troubleshoot":"If the fallback shows a message instead of a channel, inspect the alternative’s enabled flag and hours. Confirm that callback is available now and publish any corrected configuration."},
"sitewide-call-recovery":{"title":"Existing website phone links and Call Follow-up","category":"Recovery","keywords":["tel","sitewide","phone","recovery","filters","numbers"],"what":"Call Follow-up offers another contact option after a visitor taps a phone number. Existing website tel: links and floating-widget calls remain distinct sources for optional analytics; a click never establishes a call outcome.","when":"Enable it if phone numbers in your header, footer or page content should have the same fallback opportunity as widget calls.","configure":"Open Call Follow-up, choose Enable Call Follow-up and set Show after (seconds). Under Choose where the follow-up appears, select Phone numbers to match, optional Number filters (one per line), Show on pages and content, and Do not show on pages and content. Existing website phone links are included automatically subject to these filters. Existing tracking and earlier callback prompts contains the separate legacy tracking/prompt controls; enable those only when you need that earlier behavior.","default":"The optional Call Follow-up prompt is off and prepared for 10 seconds. Existing phone-link tracking and earlier callback prompts are also off. Number filtering starts at all website phone links with no page filters. Optional analytics remains separately disabled until configured and published.","example":"Offer a callback after the header’s phone link is selected, but exclude a partner phone number or a particular page.","limits":"The browser cannot detect whether a call connected, failed, was answered or was missed. Follow-up itself does not require analytics consent; optional counters still require local analytics and its configured consent. The separate earlier callback prompt requires callback requests to be enabled. None of these interactions delays the original phone link.","troubleshoot":"Confirm the link uses tel:, its number passes the include/exclude filters and the page is allowed. Check callback availability and browser return/delay behavior. For missing counts, check analytics and consent independently of prompts."},
"post-call-follow-up":{"title":"Call Follow-up: optional post-call prompt","category":"Recovery","keywords":["post call","follow up","10 seconds","session","visible","close","callback"],"what":"After someone taps a phone number on your website, you can offer another way to contact you, such as requesting a callback. We cannot tell whether a phone call connected or was answered. This feature only responds to a phone-button click.","when":"Enable it when you want a gentle second contact route without claiming to know the result of the telephone call.","configure":"Open Call Follow-up and choose Enable Call Follow-up. Set Show after (seconds), initially 10 and adjustable from 2 to 120. Choose where the follow-up appears controls Phone numbers to match, Number filters (one per line), Show on pages and content, and Do not show on pages and content. Change follow-up wording contains Follow-up heading and Follow-up message. Fixed protections are not extra switches: return-to-page handling is best effort, the prompt appears only on a visible page, is limited to at most once per browser session, keeps a visible X Close and is suppressed after an accepted callback. Existing website phone links are included automatically, subject to the number/page filters. Publish changes explicitly.","default":"Post-call follow-up is off. When enabled, the prepared delay is 10 seconds. It can appear at most once per browser session; closing/dismissing it or accepting a callback suppresses further follow-up for that session.","example":"Ten seconds after Call is selected, an eligible visible page may ask Were you able to reach us? with an invitation to leave details. Request a Callback opens the existing secured callback form and uses the same private inbox.","limits":"The page must be visible and appropriate for interaction. Returning from a phone app is best effort because browser lifecycle events vary. The original tel: navigation is never prevented or delayed. The interaction itself does not require analytics consent, while any optional tracking retains its existing consent rules. A non-identifying one-bit functional session cookie for each used/accepted flag shares suppression across tabs when browser storage is available. Pending timing/source stays in sessionStorage for same-tab navigation, with sessionStorage/memory fallbacks. If storage is blocked, session-limit enforcement is best effort. The once-per-session, accepted-callback and visible-page protections are fixed behavior, not configurable controls. Returning from the phone app is best effort, not evidence of a completed or failed call.","troubleshoot":"If no prompt appears, check its published toggle, delay, page visibility, session dismissal/accepted-callback state, callback availability and relevant recovery filters. Once-per-session suppression is intentional; use a genuinely new browser session for a fresh first-interaction test."},
"call-detection-limitations":{"title":"Browser-based call detection limits","category":"Recovery","keywords":["call detection","missed call","answered","failed","return","visibility"],"what":"A browser can observe a phone-link selection and some page visibility/return events. It cannot determine whether the telephone call connected, was answered, failed or was missed.","when":"Use this distinction when interpreting Contact Analytics and choosing recovery/follow-up wording.","configure":"Write neutral follow-up copy such as Were you able to reach us? and If not, leave your contact details. Use timing and visibility as best-effort prompts only. Keep direct telephone navigation immediate and retain an accessible manual callback route.","default":"Click counts represent selected links. Recovery and post-call follow-up never label an observed click as a failed or unanswered call. The new post-call follow-up is disabled by default.","example":"A visitor taps Call, completes a successful conversation and later returns to the page. The browser cannot distinguish this from an unanswered call; the prompt therefore asks rather than asserts.","limits":"Phone-app handoffs, browser suspension, background timer throttling and return events differ across platforms. A configured delay is not a guaranteed real-time notification while the page is hidden.","troubleshoot":"If automatic follow-up is inconsistent across devices, verify visible-page and session requirements first. Do not try to infer outcomes from missing events; retain manual callback and direct WhatsApp alternatives."},
"callback-recovery-timing":{"title":"Callback follow-up timing","category":"Recovery","keywords":["delay","return","manual","fallback","dismiss","session"],"what":"Timing chooses which browser signal may offer a sitewide callback prompt: return to the page, a delay, either signal, or manual callback only. Widget fallback separately uses supported page-return events.","when":"Choose the least intrusive signal suitable for your audience and keep direct callback available for visitors who want it.","configure":"In Call Follow-up → Existing tracking and earlier callback prompts, use When to show the earlier prompt and Earlier prompt delay (seconds) for the legacy return/delay/manual behavior. Offer an earlier callback prompt and its heading/message are separate from the new Enable Call Follow-up prompt. Under Privacy & Mail, Offer a fallback after returning to the page controls the floating-widget journey. The new Call Follow-up uses Show after (seconds), initially 10, with fixed once-per-session and accepted-callback protections.","default":"Earlier sitewide timing is Return or delay with an 8-second delay, but its tracking/prompt switch starts off. The new Call Follow-up is off with a 10-second prepared delay. Widget return fallback starts on. Dismissed automatic prompts stay closed for the browsing session.","example":"Use After returning to the page for visitors switching to their phone app, or Manual callback only for a less interruptive journey.","limits":"Allowed delay is 2–120 seconds. Browser return signals vary across apps and devices. No timing option can prove that a call failed or a message was not sent; do not phrase your prompt as though it can.","troubleshoot":"If an automatic prompt does not reappear, check session dismissal, timing, callback availability and the browser’s return behavior. Verify that manual callback still opens before changing the timing."},
"contact-insights":{"title":"Contact Analytics and local reporting","category":"Insights and reports","keywords":["analytics","counts","conversion","daily","retention","date range"],"what":"Contact Analytics stores local aggregate counts of contact-link clicks, callback form opens, accepted callbacks/enquiries and form/notification errors. The private inbox and retained-request counts are separate.","when":"Enable local analytics when aggregate activity is useful and you have configured the appropriate consent behavior for your website.","configure":"Open Contact Analytics, enable local analytics, choose Analytics consent and analytics retention, then publish. Use From, Through and Apply date range to inspect inclusive website-local dates. The accessible daily table provides the same daily data as the chart. Inspect the retained inbox and customers-still-waiting figures separately.","default":"Local analytics is off. Consent is required. Aggregate retention is 90 days, adjustable from 1 to 365 days. The initial reporting window covers seven complete local days.","example":"Compare WhatsApp and Call link selections last week, then inspect accepted callback requests divided by form opens for the displayed conversion measure.","limits":"Clicks do not prove completed calls or sent messages. Counters omit activity blocked by consent, disabled collection or retention. Date filtering supports at most 366 days. No visitor profiles or individual analytics-event history are retained.","troubleshoot":"For zero counts, check published analytics, consent and real public interactions; preview never tracks. For differences from the inbox, compare consent, deletion, storage settings, time windows and the separate retention periods."},
"analytics-consent":{"title":"Analytics consent","category":"Privacy and integrations","keywords":["consent","statistics","cmp","FCBConsent","tracking","privacy"],"what":"Analytics consent controls optional local counters and GA4 events. It is separate from the form’s consent to submit personal contact details.","when":"Keep consent-required mode when your local analytics should wait for a visitor’s permission. Review your own site requirements before changing local counters to no consent requirement.","configure":"In Contact Analytics, choose the local Analytics consent mode and publish. Connect your consent manager to window.FCBConsent.setAnalytics(true) after analytics permission and setAnalytics(false) when permission is revoked. The WordPress Consent API statistics category is supported. Keep your existing Google tag’s consent behavior configured independently.","default":"Local analytics requires consent and starts disabled. GA4 always waits for analytics consent even if local counters are configured not to require it.","example":"A consent-manager callback grants analytics via window.FCBConsent.setAnalytics(true); contact forms still ask their separate required submission-consent checkbox.","limits":"The plugin is not a consent banner and does not determine your site’s legal requirements. Changing its local consent mode does not grant permission to another tracking tool. Events blocked before consent are not replayed as a historical visitor log.","troubleshoot":"Inspect whether your consent manager actually calls the public API after permission and revocation. Check that analytics or GA4 is enabled in published settings. Preview does not send events even after consent."},
"ga4-setup":{"title":"GA4 integration and event setup","category":"Privacy and integrations","keywords":["ga4","gtag","gtm","dataLayer","generate_lead","debug"],"what":"Tracking Integrations can send consented contact events through an existing gtag function or Google Tag Manager dataLayer. It never injects a Google tracking tag.","when":"Enable it only when your website already has a working Google tag or GTM container and analytics-consent handling.","configure":"Open Tracking Integrations → Google Analytics 4. Enable Send consented contact events to your existing Google tag and choose Existing gtag function or Existing Google Tag Manager dataLayer. For GTM, create your own event triggers and GA4 event tag with the required consent settings. Connect analytics consent and optionally enable GA4 debug mode while testing, then publish.","default":"GA4 is off; gtag mode is prepared and debug mode is off. Local Contact Analytics remains independent.","example":"Observe fcb_phone_click, fcb_whatsapp_click, fcb_contact_click and fcb_callback_open for relevant interactions. generate_lead is sent after an accepted submission. Parameters are channel_type, widget_location, source_category and optional debug_mode.","limits":"Events omit customer contact values, messages, phone numbers, full page URLs and submission IDs. A click cannot prove a call or message outcome. GTM dataLayer events need your own configured GA4 tag; selecting GTM alone does not create it.","troubleshoot":"Check the existing tag/container loads, analytics consent is granted and the chosen integration matches your site. Use your tag’s debugging tools and optional plugin debug mode. Test the public widget; editor preview sends no GA4 events."},
"weekly-email-reports":{"title":"Weekly email reports","category":"Insights and reports","keywords":["weekly","report","cron","schedule","recipients","timezone"],"what":"Weekly reports compare seven complete website-local days with the preceding seven, show consented activity and retained inbox submissions, and count all retained New and Contacted requests still waiting.","when":"Enable reports when someone needs a regular review without signing in every day. Use the private preview before enabling customer-detail emails.","configure":"In Contact Analytics, configure weekly reports, up to 20 recipient emails, weekday and local time. Publish those settings. Choose Generate private report preview to review published settings, or Send non-personal sample email to check mail transport. Review the next scheduled run and last-report status.","default":"Reports are off. Monday at 09:00 in the website timezone and the administrator email are prepared. Detailed customer information is off.","example":"A Monday 09:00 report covers the seven complete local days ending Sunday and includes older retained unresolved requests in Customers Still Waiting for a Response.","limits":"WordPress cron depends on traffic or server cron, so the selected time is not guaranteed. Failed weekly sends are not automatically resent. Transport acceptance is not delivery confirmation. Analytics can be incomplete; retained counts exclude deleted or never-stored requests.","troubleshoot":"If no report arrives, confirm published report settings, recipients, the next-run/last-status display and WordPress cron. Check mail transport and spam. Generate a private preview to distinguish missing data from a sending problem."},
"customer-email-details":{"title":"Detailed customer information in emails","category":"Privacy and integrations","keywords":["personal","details","report","email","retention","privacy"],"what":"The weekly report detail option adds retained names, contact values, messages and request information to email reports. Ordinary individual submission notifications already contain customer details.","when":"Enable detailed weekly reports only when the chosen recipients need personal information for follow-up and you accept that mailbox copies have separate handling.","configure":"In Contact Analytics, explicitly enable the report’s detailed customer-information option, review recipients and publish. Generate a private report preview first. Leave the option off when aggregate counts and a private inbox link are enough.","default":"Detailed weekly reports are off. Reports otherwise contain aggregates and an administrator-only inbox link. Sample test emails contain non-personal sample counts.","example":"A service manager receiving a private report can see unresolved requests; a general performance-review recipient can instead receive counts without personal details.","limits":"Each detailed section is capped at 100 records and indicates truncation. Email recipients can retain or forward content. Deleting a WordPress enquiry or shortening retention cannot erase copies already sent to a mailbox.","troubleshoot":"If details are missing, confirm the published option and whether retained records exist in that section/window. Use the private inbox for records beyond the cap. If unexpected recipients receive details, correct recipients and detail settings before the next send."},
"privacy":{"title":"Privacy and data handling","category":"Privacy and integrations","keywords":["privacy","personal data","export","erase","consent","uninstall"],"what":"The plugin handles form contacts in a private WordPress inbox, sends submission notifications, and optionally collects aggregate analytics or sends consented GA4 events. External contact services receive visitors when they choose a link. Optional post-call follow-up uses functional browser-session suppression flags, separate from analytics.","when":"Review these settings before publishing and when changing your privacy policy or follow-up process.","configure":"In Privacy & Mail, review Privacy disclosure, Privacy policy URL, Require consent before submission, Store enquiries in the private inbox, retention, notification email and Delete plugin data on uninstall. Review analytics consent, report recipients and report details separately. Use WordPress personal-data export/erase tools for matching email enquiries; handle verified phone requests through the private inbox.","default":"Submission consent and private storage are on for new installations; analytics, GA4, reports and detailed report emails are off. Data is preserved on uninstall unless explicit deletion is enabled. Deactivation preserves data.","example":"State that submitted details are used to respond to the enquiry, link your website policy and choose a retention period that matches your team’s actual follow-up process.","limits":"The plugin does not write your policy or establish compliance. Email, CSV and external-service copies are outside later local deletion. Disabling analytics or storage does not automatically erase previously retained data. Follow-up uses a one-bit browser-session cookie without an identifier, plus sessionStorage/memory fallback. Pending timing/source is kept in sessionStorage for same-tab navigation. These functional state flags do not enable analytics; browser-blocked storage can make session enforcement best effort.","troubleshoot":"If an email privacy export omits a phone-only enquiry, verify the request through your own process and use the private inbox. Inspect retention and uninstall choices separately from consent controls."},
"enquiry-retention":{"title":"Enquiry retention and deletion","category":"Privacy and integrations","keywords":["retention","cleanup","delete","days","unresolved","storage"],"what":"Enquiry retention removes expired private inbox records. Aggregate analytics has a separate retention setting.","when":"Choose a duration that supports follow-up while avoiding unnecessary local storage of personal contact details.","configure":"In Privacy & Mail, enable or disable Store enquiries in the private inbox and set Keep enquiries for (days), from 1 to 365. Publish changes. Use Customer Enquiries to delete individual records or the explicitly confirmed delete-all action when appropriate. Review Delete plugin data on uninstall separately.","default":"New installations retain enquiries for 30 days. Disabling storage stops new records without removing existing records. Scheduled cleanup deletes expired enquiries, including unresolved ones.","example":"Review New and Contacted requests before the 30-day period ends, and resolve or export necessary business records through your own retention process.","limits":"Cleanup uses WordPress scheduled tasks and may run late on low-traffic sites. All expired statuses are eligible for deletion. Email and CSV copies are not affected. A design reset or settings import does not delete enquiries.","troubleshoot":"If expired records remain, inspect the Next cleanup diagnostic and WordPress cron. If a request vanished, review retention and manual deletion actions. Shortening retention can cause existing older records to be removed on the next cleanup."},
"import-export":{"title":"Import and export settings safely","category":"Privacy and integrations","keywords":["json","import","export","backup","reuse","destinations"],"what":"Settings JSON transfers presentation and configuration without transferring customer enquiries or analytics history.","when":"Use it to reuse a design or move a reviewed configuration between WordPress sites. It is not a full database backup.","configure":"In Tracking Integrations → Move your design safely, optionally select Include public business contact destinations and choose Download settings JSON. On the destination site, choose an Import settings JSON file up to 150 KB and select Load file into draft. Review channels, destinations, visibility and privacy, then Save draft or Publish widget.","default":"Destination export is off. Exports exclude notification addresses and report recipients and disable widget, analytics, GA4, debug and scheduled reports. Import preserves the destination site’s notification/report recipients, disables those optional services and loads an unpublished editor draft.","example":"Move a design to staging without copying phone numbers. Enter staging destinations, review the imported draft, and explicitly enable and publish the widget only when ready.","limits":"The supported file has format fcb-settings and version 2. Files larger than 150 KB or malformed settings are rejected. Files optionally containing business contact destinations are readable JSON. Import replaces the current editor configuration; review unsaved work first.","troubleshoot":"Use a fresh plugin-generated JSON file if import rejects the format. Restore missing destinations before publishing. Re-enable analytics and report settings deliberately after reviewing the destination site’s consent and recipients."},
"troubleshooting":{"title":"Troubleshooting common problems","category":"Support","keywords":["missing","cache","error","mail","mobile","help","debug"],"what":"Focused checks distinguish unpublished settings, visibility/hours, browser interactions, notification transport and optional analytics configuration.","when":"Use these checks when the preview or public widget behaves differently from your expectations.","configure":"If controls or advanced navigation seem absent, choose Show Advanced Settings first. 1. Confirm Save draft or Publish widget succeeded.\n2. Check the published enabled state, selected methods and destinations.\n3. Check device settings, Display & Visibility and Explain current draft for the exact URL.\n4. Check channel hours, timezone and alternatives.\n5. Review browser errors and clear stale page caches.\n6. For forms, inspect validation, callback availability, private records and mail transport separately.","default":"Preview links do not navigate, preview forms send nothing and preview never sends analytics. Optional collection and reports are off until enabled and published.","example":"A Call button missing only at night may be correctly hidden by its schedule; a missing method after a preset change may be inside the expandable menu.","limits":"Browser app handoffs vary. A click does not confirm a conversation. WordPress cron can run late, and transport acceptance does not establish email delivery. Contextual help is local while the public documentation address is unpublished.","troubleshoot":"For zero Contact Analytics/GA4 counts, check published opt-ins and consent, then test a public interaction. For an oversized expanded surface, reset/disable the optional panel. For form styling problems, reset the form section and inspect color contrast."},
"developer-integration":{"title":"Developer integration and verified hooks","category":"Support","keywords":["developer","hooks","filters","consent","events","constants","api"],"what":"The verified browser integration is window.FCBConsent.setAnalytics(boolean). GA4 uses existing gtag or dataLayer integrations. Documentation routing adds two explicit WordPress filters and two optional constants.","when":"Use the consent API from an existing consent-manager integration, and enable public documentation centrally only after those pages are published.","configure":"Call window.FCBConsent.setAnalytics(true) after analytics consent and false on revocation. The WordPress Consent API statistics category is also supported. Set FCB_DOCS_BASE_URL or filter fcb_documentation_base_url to change the HTTPS documentation base. Set FCB_DOCS_PUBLISHED to true or filter fcb_documentation_published to true only after publication; otherwise help links remain on admin.php?page=fcb-help#topic.","default":"Proposed future base: https://github.com/Amitoshi/floating-chat-buttons#. Public documentation routing is disabled. The plugin supplies no advertised custom submission or destination filter contract in this release.","example":"add_filter('fcb_documentation_base_url', function () { return 'https://example.com/contact-guide/'; });\nadd_filter('fcb_documentation_published', '__return_true');\nEnable the published filter only after the corresponding anchors exist at that address.","limits":"WordPress AJAX endpoints, internal classes, database tables and editor events are implementation details rather than a promised extension API. No invented webhook, CRM integration or custom callback-submission hook is documented. Consent calls do not configure your separate Google tag’s consent mode.","troubleshoot":"If help still routes locally, inspect the published constant/filter and validate the HTTPS base. If consented events are missing, check that the API exists on the public page and the configured analytics/GA4 feature is published and enabled."},
"faq":{"title":"Frequently asked questions","category":"Support","keywords":["faq","free","account","presets","missed call","email","reset"],"what":"Common answers clarify the plugin’s contact, design, storage and reporting behavior.","when":"Read this before treating a missing analytics count, changed appearance or browser handoff as a completed conversation or lost customer record.","configure":"Do I need an account? No plugin account or external chat SDK is required.\nCan I use three methods in a minimal design? Yes; a single launcher keeps selected methods in its expandable menu.\nDoes a preset replace my contact numbers? No, it changes presentation.\nDoes Reset design delete enquiries? No.\nCan callback stay open when Call is closed? Yes, callback has independent hours.\nDoes Simple mode remove other methods or advanced features? No; it hides controls and summarizes additional enabled methods.\nDoes More dial a call? No; More opens additional contact options.\nWill post-call follow-up repeat on every call? No; when enabled it is limited to at most once per browser session.","default":"The plugin starts unpublished with optional analytics and reports off. New installations have private enquiry storage on and 30-day retention. The local guide works before the public documentation is published.","example":"A visitor selects Call, returns to the page and submits a callback. You can retain and follow up the request, but the earlier click still cannot tell you whether a call connected.","limits":"Does the plugin detect missed calls? No. Does accepted mail prove delivery? No. Does local deletion remove email/CSV copies? No. Are local analytics and the inbox identical? No; consent, storage and retention differ.","troubleshoot":"Use the topic links above for a specific setting. Check the published state first, then relevant visibility/hours, validation, consent or mail configuration. Review both desktop and mobile whenever changing design."},
"future-custom-html-design":{"title":"Future custom HTML design direction","category":"Support","keywords":["future","custom html","visual","separate saved designs","publish"],"what":"A future milestone may support separately saved Visual and Custom HTML designs, each requiring an explicit publish action. This is a documentation-only direction and is not implemented in M2.2.2.","when":"Treat this as planning context for a later design capability, not as an available setting in the current plugin.","configure":"Use the existing visual controls and current draft/publish workflow in M2.2.2. There is no Custom HTML editor or active Visual/Custom mode selector to configure in this release.","default":"Only the implemented visual editor is available. No custom HTML storage, renderer or editor is added by this documentation update.","example":"A future workflow could preserve a visual design and a custom design separately, with neither replacing the published design until its own explicit Publish action is chosen.","limits":"This future direction is not a promise that Custom HTML designs can currently be saved, previewed or published. Its detailed safety and compatibility rules belong to a separate milestone.","troubleshoot":"If you need a current appearance change, use Design Studio in Advanced mode. Do not look for or attempt to enable undocumented custom HTML configuration keys."}
}
